<?xml version="1.0"?><quiz type="multichoice" id="1265" lang="E" hascredits="0"><question><text>&lt;html&gt;The larva (young, worm-shaped form) of the potato beetle doesn’t have a hard shell to protect it from birds and other predators. How does the larva keep from being eaten? &lt;/html&gt;</text><image/><imagerefid/><audio/><audiorefid/></question><answers correct="3"><answer id="1"><text>&lt;html&gt;It has a poisonous bite&lt;/html&gt;</text><image/><imagerefid/><audio/><audiorefid/></answer><answer id="2"><text>&lt;html&gt;It screams loudly&lt;/html&gt;</text><image/><imagerefid/><audio/><audiorefid/></answer><answer id="3"><text>&lt;html&gt;It covers itself in toxic goop&lt;/html&gt;</text><image/><imagerefid/><audio/><audiorefid/></answer><answer id="4"><text>&lt;html&gt;It hides underground&lt;/html&gt;</text><image/><imagerefid/><audio/><audiorefid/></answer></answers><reward><text>&lt;html&gt;It covers itself in toxic goop. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The larva makes a slimy goop that covers its body. The goop is poisonous when eaten. 
&lt;/html&gt;</text><image/><imagerefid/><audio/><audiorefid/></reward><credits/><rewardcredits/></quiz>